title AMUS5 — Constel channel: the handshake crosses a byte stream, one frame at a time
prompt Keaton set the recursion loop on the 1024-round itinerary and six-season double-seat: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, self-approve design rounds, make progress before checking in, send often as its own signed round, stop at custody gates.
think AMUS4 (exchange) landed and sent this turn — two piers agree over whole frames. The road-on named AMUS5 as the in-process byte queue. Lindy-first crux-first: AMUS5 is the LAST purely-local rung before the socket, and it isolates the one hard thing a real transport adds — a byte stream — into a bounded value proven on the bench, so AMUS6 becomes the same channel backed by a file descriptor rather than a new design under pressure.
think The genuinely new concern (nothing before it had this): a frame may split across two arrivals, and two frames may sit back to back. read_frame is where the stream discipline lives — read the declared plen from the header at the cursor, refuse NeedMoreBytes when the header or payload tail has not arrived (WITHOUT moving the cursor, so the caller retries), refuse BadFrame on a wild length before any over-read, and advance the cursor by exactly one whole frame. The channel only delimits; the caller runs wire.deframe_* so the AMUS3 gates (Sha256 + naming-law border) stay the only trust boundary.
obs A red caught on metal: the overfull test pushed three greeting frames and expected ChannelFull, but max_channel is 2*max_frame=252 bytes while a real greeting frame is only ~64 bytes — the buffer holds ~3.9 of them, so three did not overflow. Fixed by pushing in a bounded loop (guard = max_channel/header_len + 2, strictly more than the buffer can ever hold) and asserting the refusal arrives within that bound — a measurement-beats-memory fix rather than a magic count.
obs Witness proves on metal across six blocks: a whole exchange completes over two channels (xnb and xnc through byte queues alone); a partial frame refuses NeedMoreBytes without moving the cursor and completes on its last byte; two back-to-back frames read one at a time, exact and in order, a third waiting; a wild declared length refuses BadFrame; an overfull channel refuses ChannelFull; and a frame read cleanly off the stream but carrying a smuggled vowel still refuses VowelPresent at the border. Witness GREEN, TAME bans clean, width exit 0; prior AMUS rungs stay GREEN (additive).
obs Kept docs synced (docs-implementation-sync): constel/README.md gains the channel.rye rung, the run block lists amus_channel_witness, Status and Kin weave include AMUS5, and the road-on now names ONLY AMUS6 (the real Comlink socket) as the remaining rung — and marks it plainly as the one that stops for Keaton's word because it crosses the Comlink seam and touches seed/comlink/.
loom witness=amus_channel_witness blocks=6 max_channel_bytes=252 greeting_frame_bytes=64 wall_ms_note=selftest under a second after build
